I completely understand the frustration. When a DataGrid goes completely blank after an API switch, it means we have a disconnect between what the backend is sending and what the frontend expects.
There are exactly **two reasons** why you aren't seeing a single request right now:
 1. **Legacy Data Filtered Out:** The new SQL query uses an INNER JOIN on the VOUCHER_POSTING table. Any old batches you created *before* we built this workflow engine will not show up because they don't have workflow states!
 2. **Role ID Extraction Failure:** If your jwtUtil fails to parse the new 9001 ID properly, the backend defaults to "OTHER" and returns 0 rows.
 3. **JSON Key Mismatch:** My previous backend map used "totalRows", but your frontend DataGrid is still looking for field: "requestCount".
Let's fix this immediately so you can see your data and test the Stepper.
### The Bulletproof Fix (Update your Java Backend)
We are going to update the getPendingBatchesForChecker method in your VoucherRequestServiceImpl.java.
This update fixes the JSON keys to perfectly match your React frontend, and **temporarily relaxes the strict Role filter** so you can see *all* pending batches and verify the Stepper works.
**REPLACE YOUR ENTIRE METHOD WITH THIS:**
```java
    @Override
    public List<Map<String, Object>> getPendingBatchesForChecker(Integer userRole) {
        
        // 1. Fix the SQL to show ALL pending batches right now so we can test the Stepper UI
        String sql = """
            SELECT 
                b.BATCH_ID, p.VID_FK, b.CREATOR_ID, b.REQ_DATE, b.BATCH_REMARKS, 
                b.TOTAL_ROWS, p.CURRENT_STATUS, p.NEXT_APPROVAL, p.SCHEDULED_TIME
            FROM JOURNAL_BATCH_MASTER b
            INNER JOIN VOUCHER_POSTING p ON b.BATCH_ID = p.BATCH_ID
            WHERE p.CURRENT_STATUS LIKE 'PENDING_%'
            ORDER BY b.REQ_DATE DESC
        """;

        return jdbcTemplate.query(sql, (rs, rowNum) -> {
            Map<String, Object> map = new HashMap<>();
            
            // 2. FIXED: These keys now EXACTLY match your React DataGrid 'field' names
            map.put("batchId", rs.getString("BATCH_ID"));
            map.put("creatorId", rs.getString("CREATOR_ID"));
            map.put("requestDate", rs.getTimestamp("REQ_DATE"));
            map.put("requestCount", rs.getLong("TOTAL_ROWS")); // Mapped from TOTAL_ROWS to requestCount
            map.put("workflowState", rs.getString("CURRENT_STATUS")); // Feeds the Stepper!
            
            return map;
        }); 
    }

```
